WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:02.133
Standard deviation.

2
00:00:02.433 --> 00:00:05.233
One number tells you how far from normal is too far.

3
00:00:06.433 --> 00:00:09.600
Standard deviation is the square root of the variance.

4
00:00:09.900 --> 00:00:12.634
That puts spread back in the original units.

5
00:00:12.634 --> 00:00:17.233
A sigma of point three kelvin on a temperature channel means exactly that.

6
00:00:17.533 --> 00:00:21.733
A z-score then asks: how many standard deviations out is this value?

7
00:00:22.933 --> 00:00:26.900
For Gaussian data, sigma slices the bell into predictable bands.

8
00:00:27.200 --> 00:00:31.200
About sixty eight percent of values fall within one sigma of the mean.

9
00:00:31.500 --> 00:00:36.333
Ninety five percent within two sigma, and ninety nine point seven percent within three.

10
00:00:37.533 --> 00:00:39.667
The z-score is a simple formula.

11
00:00:39.967 --> 00:00:42.567
On top, how far the value sits from the mean.

12
00:00:42.867 --> 00:00:47.300
Underneath, divide by sigma, so the answer is in standard deviations.

13
00:00:47.600 --> 00:00:50.833
Do this to every feature and they share comparable units.

14
00:00:52.033 --> 00:00:53.567
Let's check the rule in code.

15
00:00:53.867 --> 00:00:57.900
We generate one hundred thousand noise readings with a sigma of one.

16
00:00:58.200 --> 00:01:01.433
Then count how many fall within one, two, and three sigma.

17
00:01:01.733 --> 00:01:05.867
Out come sixty eight, ninety five, and ninety nine point seven percent.

18
00:01:07.067 --> 00:01:09.533
Now the real use: anomaly detection.

19
00:01:09.833 --> 00:01:12.133
A reading arrives on the telemetry stream.

20
00:01:12.433 --> 00:01:15.767
We compute its z-score against a rolling mean and sigma.

21
00:01:16.067 --> 00:01:22.467
Beyond three sigma happens by chance only about point three percent of the time, so it's probably not chance.

22
00:01:22.767 --> 00:01:26.400
To limit false alarms, we page only after two in a row.

23
00:01:27.600 --> 00:01:29.533
Here's the lesson from that pipeline.

24
00:01:29.833 --> 00:01:32.300
Each channel keeps a rolling mean and sigma.

25
00:01:32.600 --> 00:01:37.467
On battery temperature, chance alone fires about three alerts per thousand readings.

26
00:01:37.767 --> 00:01:41.233
So choosing the threshold is choosing your false alarm budget.

27
00:01:42.433 --> 00:01:43.178
Let's recap.

28
00:01:43.178 --> 00:01:46.900
Sigma is the square root of variance, in the original units.

29
00:01:47.200 --> 00:01:54.500
For Gaussian data, sixty eight, ninety five, and ninety nine point seven percent fall within one, two, and three sigma.

30
00:01:54.800 --> 00:01:56.500
A z-score counts the sigmas.

31
00:01:56.800 --> 00:01:59.958
And your threshold sets your false alarm budget.

32
00:01:59.958 --> 00:02:01.800
Next up: Hypothesis testing.
